<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Helvetic Confession</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1566">1566</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Helvetic Confession is a Reformed statement of faith written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-bullinger">Heinrich Bullinger</a> and published in 1566, after it was adopted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a> Frederick III. Accepted by the Swiss Reformed churches and by Reformed churches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and elsewhere, it became one of the most widely recognised confessions of Reformed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-bullinger">Heinrich Bullinger</a>, the Swiss Reformed theologian who had also helped draw up the First Helvetic Confession at Basel in 1536, wrote the Second Helvetic Confession in 1562 and revised it in 1564 as a private exercise. It came to the notice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-elector-palatine">Frederick III, Elector Palatine</a>, who had it translated into German and published. Some Reformed leaders welcomed it as a corrective to what they saw as the overly Lutheran statements of the Strasbourg Consensus.</p><p>Early in 1566 an attempt was made to have all the churches of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> sign it as a common statement of faith. The Swiss churches, which had found the First Confession too short and too Lutheran, received it well, although the Basel clergy declined to sign, preferring to keep their own Basel Confession of 1534. On the strength of this near-universal approval Frederick presented it to the Diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> of 1566 as an authoritative statement of the international Reformed faith.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The confession opens with Scripture as the sole infallible authority, its own interpreter, against which all other human writings are judged. It confesses the Trinity and accepts the Apostles' Creed, condemns images of God or Christ and rejects the invocation of saints while honouring them as examples of faith.</p><p>Its chapter on predestination has been read as moderate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a> or Augustinianism. It rejects confession to a priest and the doctrines of penance and indulgences, teaches an invisible Church known only to God, and holds that presbyter and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> were one office in the apostolic Church. It counts two sacraments, condemns Eucharistic adoration of the Mass, and treats the civil magistrate as God's servant, condemning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabaptism">Anabaptists</a>, who held that a Christian should not hold civil office.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Reformed churches adopted the confession throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> in 1566, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in 1567, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1571 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1578. According to Dezső Buzogány, in 17th- and 18th-century Hungary it was central to the training of future clergy, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-catechism">Heidelberg Catechism</a> taught congregations and children. In 1791 the Hungarian Reformed Synod of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> ordered schools to teach it as part of the official curriculum. In 1967 it was included in the Book of Confessions of the United Presbyterian Church in the U.S.A., and it remains in that of the Presbyterian Church (U.S.A.).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Helvetic Confessions” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successor Heinrich Bullinger, head of the Zürich church from 1531 to 1575, corresponded across Europe, agreed the Consensus Tigurinus with John Calvin in 1549 and wrote the <b>Second Helvetic Confession</b> of 1566, adopted by Reformed churches from Hungary to Scotland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
